ive cameras  other neutrino detectors follow similar  designs but are filled with different  kinds of liquid which absorb different  energies of neutrinos  when a neutrino collides with one of the  electrons in the liquid the electron  absorbs their energy and whizzes off  through the liquid giving out a special  kind of light called cherenkov radiation  cherenkov radiation is kind of like a  sonic boom but for light because the  electrons travel faster than light does  in the liquid  which sounds weird because it sounds  like i'm saying electrons travel faster  than light well yes they do but only  because light slows down in a liquid  the electrons don't go faster than light  in a vacuum that's the thing that's  impossible but you can go faster than  light if you slow down light enough like  in a liquid and in that case you'll emit  this cherenkov radiation  so with these massive vats of liquid  people are seeing the sporadic neutrino  impacts collecting data and trying to  figure out how neutrinos really work  which is all good science and all that  but i was thinking these detectors look  kind of like big eyes  if you think about it our eyes are vats  of liquid and your retinas are sensitive  to light like the photomultipliers  so if a neutrino hit an electron in your  eye would it also create cherenkov  radiation and could you see it  of course our eyes are way smaller than  these giant detectors but we have a lot  of eyes  so i tried to figure it all out with  maths and i also ran it past a professor  of these things and yeah there's a very  small chance a neutrino will collide  with your eye gloop and create a flash  of light  but this will only happen to you on  average once every 89 000 years  so you'll probably never see one but  there are a lot of human eyes on earth  in fact if you collected all the human  eyeballs on earth right now you could  fill up super cameo candy twice over  and that's why i love physics  anyway averaging all this out over the  7.